=====================================================================
1. THE DESIGN AS DRAWN
=====================================================================
Footprint 22 x 22 mm; total height 2.5 mm; bottom BGA solder ball array.

Layer stack (top to bottom):
1. Top EMI-shielded encapsulation lid with thermal dissipation vents.
2. High-Z radiation attenuation shield layer — tungsten-copper / tantalum
   graded alloy, printed density rho = 17,850 kg/m3, CTE 4.3 ppm/K.
3. Active Silicon / GaN die with micro-bump interconnects.
4. Phonon-tuned multi-layer ceramic & metal-matrix substrate —
   diamond-copper (Dia-Cu) heat spreader, 550 W/mK, CTE 4.1 ppm/K;
   CTE matched; acoustic impedance rule Z = rho * v.
5. Bottom BGA solder ball array.

EDA / CAD simulation & material filtering rules (printed on sheet 1):
- Density Guardrail (Engineering of the Blackwell Minerals Conservation
  Law): 500.0957 <= rho <= 22604.3007 kg/m3 for every material in the stack.
- CTE mismatch limit: delta_alpha < 0.5 ppm/K.
- Radiation dose attenuation: > 99.4% @ 100 MeV (sheet 1) — see Section 3.
- Thermal conductivity: > 220 W/mK.

Printed thermal register (sheet 2): T_die = 373 K, T_Dia-Cu = 340 K,
T_shield = 320 K, T_lid = 305 K; overall thermal resistance
R_theta = 0.18 K/W; max die temperature 373 K (operating).

=====================================================================
2. ENGINE RUN — WHAT CARRIES (EQS-1212)
=====================================================================
GUARDRAIL LEG — CARRIES.
The shield alloy at rho = 17,850 kg/m3 prices through the filed seat
function seat(rho) = 18.319261532267927 + (1/4) ln(rho/5514) to
seat 18.6129 — stratum SHELL, inside the Blackwell Minerals Conservation
Law band [500.0957, 22604.3007], under the ceiling constraint the sheet
itself prints. The alloy is compositionally reachable from labelled
handbook densities (W 19,250 / Ta 16,650 / Cu 8,960): a W-rich graded
blend with Cu binder class sits at 17,850. The minerals law functions
in this design as a CAD material filter — a specification applied
before fabrication.

THERMAL LEG — REGISTER CONSISTENT AS PRINTED, NOT MEASURED.
The printed ladder (373 / 340 / 320 / 305 K, deltaT die-to-lid 68 K)
with R_theta = 0.18 K/W implies 377.8 W conducted through the package.
The Dia-Cu substrate conduction floor computes to 0.0038 K/W
(1 mm at 550 W/mK over 22x22 mm) and does not contradict the claimed
0.18 K/W. The register is internally coherent; it is a computed
specification, not a measurement.

=====================================================================
3. ENGINE RUN — THE TWO GATES (priced, with their fixes)
=====================================================================
GATE 1 — ATTENUATION AT 100 MeV.
The two sheets disagree on their faces: sheet 1 prints > 99.4%,
sheet 2 prints 99.7%. At 100 MeV neither figure is entailed by the
geometry. Labelled inputs (PSTAR class, W-class at 100 MeV): CSDA mass
range 10.6 g/cm2; entrance mass stopping power 4.4 MeV cm2/g. The 2.5 mm
stack affords at most ~1 mm of shield; 1 mm of the 17,850 alloy removes
~7.9 MeV from a 100 MeV proton, which exits at ~92.1 MeV.
FIX (either): (a) deepen the shield to ~5.9 mm of the alloy along the
particle path (total stack ~7.5 mm) for a true stopping claim at
100 MeV; or (b) re-scope the rated particle energy — at 10 MeV the
shield as drawn stops the proton many times over.

GATE 2 — CTE AT THE DIE INTERFACE.
Printed layers: shield 4.3 vs Dia-Cu 4.1 ppm/K = 0.2 apart — inside the
0.5 limit against each other. Against the die they are not: silicon
(2.6 ppm/K) stands 1.5 from the Dia-Cu; GaN (5.6 ppm/K) stands 1.5.
FIX: print the graded die-face CTE the phonon-tuned stack must deliver
(~3.0 ppm/K class for a silicon die face) as a specified interface
value; the grading profile that achieves it is a design specification
to be added to the sheets.

